import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse('{"title":"ONNX as a second backend, in two directions","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"ONNX as a second backend, in two directions","type":"adr","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z","status":"accepted","status_note":"**Accepted (2026-10-15) — direction 1 shipped, and the boundary\\nheld.**  `packages/catopt-onnx` implements `Sink` over 169 IR ops\\n(opset 18), verified through `onnxruntime` (219 tests; ~150\\nprograms round-tripped).  Pipeline round-trip:\\n`Linear→ReLU→Linear→softmax` 1.49e-08 (fp32), 3× stacked `Linear`\\n**1.11e-16** (fp64, machine epsilon), a tuple-returning two-head\\nmodule < 1e-6 on both outputs.\\n\\n**The falsification did not fire**: `git diff --name-only` against\\nthe package list shows **zero** changes under `catopt-core` (or any\\nother package) — the claim *\\"a new backend implements `Sink`;\\nnothing in core changes\\"* held on the first non-torch backend.\\n\\n**What the experiment actually found** (the valuable part):\\n\\n- Two port surfaces are thinner than a second backend needs, both\\n  worked around sink-side rather than by widening core: `Source`\\n  carries **no element type** for inputs (`Var.typ` is shape-only,\\n  so an index input is untypable without a dtype channel), and\\n  `Capabilities.ops` is spelled `torch_bindings` (cosmetic — the\\n  pipeline never reads it).\\n- `supported_ops` is a *name* set, but ONNX feasibility is\\n  sometimes **attribute-dependent** (a `transpose` needs a static\\n  rank); those refuse at lowering rather than at pricing time, so\\n  extraction can in principle commit to a form the sink then\\n  declines — no occurrence in the tests, but a real seam.\\n- Two attribute-spelling traps, exactly as predicted: ONNX\\n  `HardSigmoid` defaults `alpha=0.2` where ATen is `1/6` (silent\\n  20% slope error), and `Flatten` is always 2-D.\\n- fp64 is exact only where onnxruntime ships a double kernel\\n  (measured gaps: `Tan`, `Asin`, `Acos`, `Atan`, `Sinh`, `Cosh`,\\n  `Erf`, `Conv`, `InstanceNormalization`).\\n- Only `Monolithic` is exercised: `Compositional` needs a\\n  `Composer`, `Autotuned` a `Meter` — both torch-shaped ports with\\n  no ONNX equivalent.  **That is the datum for direction 2.**\\n\\n**Open follow-ups:** a staged coverage climb for `catopt-onnx`\\n(not yet in `[tool.coverage.run] source`); direction 2\\n(`OnnxSource`) remains unbuilt."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/adrs/0006-onnx-backend.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/adrs/0006-onnx-backend.md"}'),a={name:`graphopt/project/adrs/0006-onnx-backend.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="adr-0006-—-onnx-as-a-second-backend-in-two-directions" tabindex="-1">ADR 0006 — ONNX as a second backend, in two directions <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-0006-—-onnx-as-a-second-backend-in-two-directions" aria-label="Permalink to “ADR 0006 — ONNX as a second backend, in two directions”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Date</strong>: 2026-10-15 (updated same day). <strong>Depends on</strong>: ADR 0003 (evaluation as an independent dimension), ADR 0005 (the open game). <strong>Related</strong>: <code>catopt_core.ports</code> (<code>Source</code>/<code>Sink</code>), <code>catopt_torch.adapters</code> (the reference backend), <code>catopt_core.attrs.ATTR_SCHEMA</code>, the <code>pack</code> op (multi-output).</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>Every model the optimizer has touched entered through <code>TorchSource</code> (<code>torch.export</code> → IR) and left through <code>TorchSink</code> (IR → <code>IRModule</code>). The hexagonal boundary was built for more than one backend — <code>AGENTS.md</code> states it outright: <em>&quot;A new backend implements <code>Sink</code>; nothing in <code>catopt-core</code> changes.&quot;</em> — and <code>import-linter</code> enforces it (core imports no torch, numpy, or GPU library; adapters push state into core through registered seams).</p><p>ONNX is the obvious second backend: it is the interchange format every other framework speaks (TF, JAX, Paddle, TFLite, the hardware vendors&#39; compilers), and the IR is already backend-neutral — <code>catopt_core</code> has no tensor library in it.</p><p><strong>Today ONNX is explicitly unsupported</strong>, and pinned as such: <code>tests/test_export.py::test_unknown_format_error</code> asserts <code>export_optimized(..., fmt=&quot;onnx&quot;)</code> raises <code>unsupported export format</code>. That is the honest current state, not an accident.</p><p>The pressure this ADR answers: <em>is ONNX a second backend we adopt, and in which direction first?</em> The two directions have very different costs and different value.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Adopt ONNX as a backend, staged: export first, import second.</strong> Core does not change; ONNX lands as its own package behind the existing ports, with the op-mapping work made explicit rather than discovered late.</p><h3 id="direction-1-—-export-ir-→-onnx-—-the-first-stage" tabindex="-1">Direction 1 — export (IR → ONNX) — the first stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#direction-1-—-export-ir-→-onnx-—-the-first-stage" aria-label="Permalink to “Direction 1 — export (IR → ONNX) — the first stage”">​</a></h3><p><code>OnnxSink</code> implements <code>Sink</code>: IR → an ONNX graph (via <code>onnx</code> / <code>onnxruntime</code> for verification). This is the <em>inverse</em> of what <code>torch_bridge</code> already does — a table from the IR op set to ONNX ops plus the attribute spelling each op uses.</p><p>Value: an optimized program can be delivered to any ONNX runtime — the hardware-vendor compilers, mobile, non-torch serving — which is where the <em>measured</em> wins could actually reach users. Cost: moderate. The op table is finite and the IR is already the intermediate form; the risk is attribute spelling (see the positional-attr trap below), not architecture.</p><h3 id="direction-2-—-import-onnx-→-ir-—-the-second-stage" tabindex="-1">Direction 2 — import (ONNX → IR) — the second stage <a class="header-anchor" href="#direction-2-—-import-onnx-→-ir-—-the-second-stage" aria-label="Permalink to “Direction 2 — import (ONNX → IR) — the second stage”">​</a></h3><p><code>OnnxSource</code> implements <code>Source</code>: an ONNX graph → IR. This is what lets the optimizer touch models that are not torch at all.</p><p>Cost: <strong>the large one</strong>, for three reasons measured on the torch side:</p><ol><li><strong>The op-mapping long tail.</strong> The torch backend needed ~260 bindings, an <code>ATTR_SCHEMA</code> for positional→named attributes, and per-op shape rules. ONNX needs the same three artifacts for its op set — mechanical, but the long tail is where the bugs live: the U²-Net trial found <code>max_pool2d</code> silently truncating because <code>ceil_mode</code> arrives <em>positionally</em>, and <code>upsample_bilinear2d</code> placing <code>align_corners</code> at position 2 so the scale lands at 3 (committed <code>ecb2fb7</code>).</li><li><strong>Shape inference.</strong> The IR&#39;s typing is explicit; ONNX graphs carry less of it, and several laws (the carrier/<code>_vshape</code> family) are shape-guarded. Without a shape source the interesting half of the library is unreachable.</li><li><strong>Opset drift.</strong> ONNX ops change semantics across opsets; the mapping must pin the opset it claims to support.</li></ol><h3 id="prerequisite-already-met-multi-output" tabindex="-1">Prerequisite already met: multi-output <a class="header-anchor" href="#prerequisite-already-met-multi-output" aria-label="Permalink to “Prerequisite already met: multi-output”">​</a></h3><p>ONNX graphs are natively multi-output, and until this week the torch path silently delivered <strong>one</strong> output (<code>export_to_ir</code> took the first resolvable leaf and <code>break</code>ed; measured on U²-Net: 7 outputs, 1 delivered). The <code>pack</code> op + <code>IR.roots</code> + per-output verification (branch <code>feat/multi-output</code>) close that — a second backend would otherwise have inherited the same bug.</p><h3 id="the-interim-refuse-do-not-drop" tabindex="-1">The interim: refuse, do not drop <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-interim-refuse-do-not-drop" aria-label="Permalink to “The interim: refuse, do not drop”">​</a></h3><p>Until <code>OnnxSink</code> exists, the honest behaviour is what ships today: an explicit <code>unsupported export format</code> error. A silent single-output delivery is worse than a refusal, and the same rule applies to any ONNX op the mapping cannot express — decline the model, never drop a node.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The boundary is validated by use.</strong> The first non-torch backend is the real test of ADR 0003&#39;s claim that semantics/search/evaluation/execution are independent: if ONNX needs core changes, the claim was overstated and this ADR records the falsification.</li><li><strong>Verification semantics must be pinned.</strong> The equivalence oracle is numeric on real tensors; for ONNX the comparison runs through <code>onnxruntime</code>, so tolerance and dtype policy need stating, and the certificate&#39;s meaning (a replayable derivation over IR terms) stays backend-independent — that part does not move.</li><li><strong>Costing stays backend-aware.</strong> <code>backend_cost(cost_fn, sink.supported_ops)</code> already prices only what a backend can lower; an ONNX backend narrows or widens that set, and the search follows automatically.</li><li><strong>The staged order is a commitment.</strong> Export-first is not timidity — it is where the value is (delivery to other runtimes) at a fraction of the cost, and it produces the op table the import direction then reuses in reverse.</li><li><strong>What this is not.</strong> Not a rewrite, not a new IR, not a second semantics. The IR stays the interchange form; ONNX is a pair of adapters behind ports that already exist.</li></ul><h2 id="direction-2-—-onnxsource-implemented" tabindex="-1">Direction 2 — <code>OnnxSource</code> (implemented) <a class="header-anchor" href="#direction-2-—-onnxsource-implemented" aria-label="Permalink to “Direction 2 — OnnxSource (implemented)”">​</a></h2><p><code>OnnxSource</code> implements <code>Source</code>: <code>onnx.ModelProto</code> (or a <code>.onnx</code> path) → <code>(IR, leaves)</code>, over <code>catopt_onnx.liftmap</code>&#39;s <code>ONNX_LIFTS</code> table — <strong>107 op types</strong>, the inverse vocabulary of the sink&#39;s <code>ONNX_BINDINGS</code>, sharing <code>errors.py</code>&#39;s refusal vocabulary (<code>UnsupportedOnnxOpError</code> for op-level refusals, new <code>OnnxImportError</code> for structural ones: opsets outside the pinned 13–21 window, non-tensor inputs/outputs, missing producers).</p><p><strong>What the direction actually costs.</strong> The ADR&#39;s three predicted costs all showed up, two smaller than feared, one exactly as predicted:</p><ol><li><em>The op-mapping long tail</em> — real but bounded: ONNX moves its op parameters from attributes to <em>tensor inputs</em> (opset 13+: <code>Slice</code> bounds, <code>Reduce*</code> axes, <code>Clip</code> limits, <code>Reshape</code> shapes), so the importer&#39;s real work is <strong>attr folding</strong> — initializer/<code>Constant</code> inputs become IR attrs under <code>ATTR_SCHEMA</code>&#39;s canonical spellings, and a computed (non-constant) parameter input is a refusal. That is the direction-2 form of &quot;ambiguous attribute&quot;: the value is data, not metadata.</li><li><em>Shape inference</em> — <code>onnx.shape_inference</code> runs once and types every leaf; <code>dim_param</code> dims land as the IR&#39;s <code>None</code> extent (representable), while a value with no tensor type at all is a refusal.</li><li><em>Opset drift</em> — pinned to a window (13–21) the way the sink pins 18; outside is refused, not guessed.</li></ol><p><strong>The port gap, worked around source-side.</strong> <code>Source</code> returns <code>(IR, leaves)</code> and <code>Var.typ</code> is shape-only — ONNX inputs <em>do</em> declare element types, and the IR has no channel for them. The workaround is the sink&#39;s, mirrored: <code>OnnxSource.input_dtypes</code> publishes the declared per-input dtypes after each import, and the caller wires it back (<code>OnnxSink(input_dtypes=source.input_dtypes)</code>) so an <code>int64</code> index input stays an index input. Zero core changes — the <em>falsification did not fire a second time</em>: <code>git diff</code> shows no edits under <code>catopt-core</code>.</p><p><strong>Second new seam found.</strong> <code>Op</code> has no name field, so ONNX node-name provenance survives only on <code>Var</code>/<code>Param</code> leaves (which keep the graph&#39;s own value names) and in refusal messages. Also, <code>Source.to_ir(model, example_inputs)</code>&#39;s second argument has no role for ONNX — the graph declares its own shapes — it is accepted and ignored so the <code>Optimizer</code> call shape stays uniform.</p><p><strong>Multi-output held both ways.</strong> Graph outputs become <code>IR.roots</code> via <code>pack</code> exactly as the torch source mints them; a <code>Split</code>&#39;s outputs mint one <code>split(index=i)</code> projection per live output name. Verified end-to-end: torch → IR → ONNX → IR → optimizer → onnxruntime — <code>Linear→ReLU→Linear→softmax</code> verified <code>passed</code> (max_abs &lt; 1e-5 vs the original torch module), and a shipped law (<code>silu_fold</code>) fires on the lifted term and wins extraction.</p>`,29)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};